00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:06.230 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this week nfl players reported to  training camp the kansas city chiefs are  set to defend their super bowl  championship but they will be without  their starting lineman  laurent duvernay tardif who announced he  is sitting out this season  he's just the second medical school  graduate to compete in the nfl  and spent nine weeks in the off season  helping out in his hometown of montreal  during the height of the covet 19  outbreak  it was kind of a crazy off season you  know you win the super bowl  and then everything is shut down it's a  pandemic it was the culmination of a  dream  for kansas city chief's offensive  lineman laurent duvernay tardif  winning the super bowl back in february  it was an amazing moment we won in miami  48 hours later we had the parade in  kansas city where like a million people  showed up and it was just amazing  to be able to do that with your friends  your family the rest of the celebration  like vacation with his longtime  girlfriend was cut short  news of the coronavirus spread sent them  back to quebec canada  their off-season home once you were back  how much thought did it take  for you in deciding that you wanted to  help out in some way  well at the beginning i feel like for  everybody it was like how do we manage  it personally everything is canceled and  you're kind of frustrated about the  situation  but at some point you realize i'm  privileged you know i still have a job  you know i'm well my family is safe  so then you start thinking all right how  can i contribute how  how can i help laurent was in a unique  position to help  having already graduated from medical  school  drafted by the chiefs in 2014 he'd  gotten cooperation from the team  and montreal's prestigious mcgill  medical school to follow a four-year  curriculum where he'd do four months of  study  while playing nfl football when i walk  on that stage in 2018 and got that md  [Music]  it was one of the best moments of my  life and then once i  lived at that lombardi trophy i feel  like i was able to  combine my true passion at the highest  level laurent traces his sense of  curiosity and adventure back to his  childhood  on extended sailing trips with his  family he fell in love with football as  a teenager  but in academics medicine was not his  first love  i was more focused on engineering but i  realize quickly you gotta be good with  your computer and i'm not really good  with technology  [Laughter]  so i kind of felt like medicine was the  perfect balance between  human attraction and science while  laurent  was able to balance football and  medicine while getting his md  playing in the nfl kept him from doing  his residency  without it he couldn't work as a doctor  in a hospital  even in a pandemic but he did get a  chance to use his training  working in a long-term care facility did  you hesitate at all  no if you think there was a crisis in  the general population  i mean in inside those long-term care  facilities it was 10 times worse so they  needed people to go there and help  and it's only when when i got the call  saying you're starting tomorrow  that i actually start asking myself  question like  am i allowed to do that with my nfl  contract uh how is it gonna play out  with my girlfriend  with my parents how we're going to  manage it this apartment was  part of managing it as far as keeping  his family safe  laurent used it as a transitional zone  after every shift to shower and wash his  clothes before going back home  as for the nfl risk he turned to chief's  head coach andy reed  whose mother by coincidence was also a  mcgill med school graduate  he said like do what you got to do like  you've worked all those years for a  reason  and right now that's the the best thing  you can do is to go back and help and  i remember him saying like this is this  is actually bigger than football  it's about how can you help and save  life or at least be part of a team who  would do so so for nine weeks laurent  followed his coach's advice  working as what he called a nurse slash  doctor slash orderly  what was the hardest thing for you  working in this facility when i first  started  at the beginning i was really focused on  like  all right give me a task and i'm going  to do it to the best of my ability you  know whether it's  passing medication uh changing diapers  feeding people  i quickly realized there's a difference  between treating  and caring for for a patient it's about  making them comfortable and also making  sure that they preserve  their dignity and for me working in in  this environment  i feel like my mindset really changed  and that is when he became vulnerable  that's when you start  interacting with human being instead of  sick patients  and when you do that that's really  taking a toll on you emotionally because  you're taking your phone you're  facetiming you know a patient's family  and you see  the whole family you know crying in  front of their computer because it's the  first  time that they actually seen their  father you know for the past  eight weeks it wasn't all tears  laurent was able to bring some joy using  his nfl status and contacts  i reached out to two of my friends that  actually played for the montreal  canadien and i was like guys  can you send me two jerseys and can we  face time after my shift at three  o'clock  and you're gonna make two patients so  happy and that's what we did and it was  an amazing experience  for the patient but also just for the  personnel in general like those guys  were amazing and they took the time to  like  talk to the nurse and talk to the people  and i think that that was kind of my job  going in there  once again combining his two passions  football  and medicine those nine weeks have been  so  rich uh to a certain extent and and so  emotionally challenging and i feel like  people that i've been working in those  facilities for the past 30 years  we owe them a lot and right now more  than ever some doctors some nurses are  living in like  trailer park because they don't want to  infect their family or bring it back  home to their kids  and to see that people are coming every  day  with a smile on their face trying to  bring joy  and do good it's really touching  you haven't gone through your residency  yet but it seems like this  is already making you a better doctor  yeah for sure  it's been quite an experience to be  honest it was not an easy one but i  think i learned a lot  learning how to work with the the team  around you there's no more  hierarchy it's everybody working  together  in order to better care for the patient  last week laura announced he would sit  out the 2020 nfl season  in a statement he wrote i cannot allow  myself to potentially transmit the virus  in our communities  simply to play the sport that i love if  i am to take risks  i will do it caring for patients  when we spoke in june he had been  planning to return  but that eventual final choice between  football and medicine  was something already looming how much  longer do you play football and when do  you focus  on medicine instead honestly i don't  know i don't have that answer  i always told myself you know after a  super bowl maybe i'm going to call it a  day but  uh you can't now i can't i just love  football i still want to be on that  field i still want to  you know go and win it again and it's my  passion you know and i feel like once  you  once you stop playing football there's  no going back i can be a doctor for the  next 30 years but  if i can be a football player for the  next two three four years i think i'm  gonna be a  happy man you know after talking with  him it didn't surprise me last week to  hear that decision it was really obvious  that this had a huge impact on him and i  do believe he wants to play football  again  right now he's trying to consider what  he's going to do perhaps he could work  in medicine this off season he has a  great foundation with kids that he can  work with too  um but it's just important to remember  this was his decision we talked about  the nba players  his personal decision and we hope to  hear from him again to see exactly what  he will be doing  you can tell how much he's thought about  all this yeah so much i don't know how  you would go through that  and not be changed even as a doctor but  what a great way to start this medical  career  that he has and also the way he talks  about the other doctors and nurses who  are showing up at these places the joy  they have in knowing that they're  helping right and taking  care of these especially elderly  patients in these long-term care  facilities who that's what matters their  dignity at the end of the day i'm trying  to talk to relatives

